changeSet.sql:
WITH new_course AS (
    INSERT INTO courses (name, description, created_time, updated_time)
    VALUES ('Java Developer', 'Learn Java fundamentals, control flow, and object-oriented programming through explanations and practical exercises.', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP)
    RETURNING id
), new_chapters AS (
    INSERT INTO chapter (name, description, sort_order, course_id, created_time, updated_time)
    SELECT data.name, data.description, data.position, new_course.id, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
    FROM new_course CROSS JOIN (VALUES
        ('Control Flow', 'Make decisions with boolean expressions and repeat operations with loops.', 1),
        ('Object-Oriented Programming', 'Model application data and behavior using classes and objects.', 2)
    ) AS data(name, description, position)
    RETURNING id, sort_order
)
INSERT INTO lesson (name, description, content, sort_order, chapter_id, created_time, updated_time)
SELECT data.name, data.description, data.content, data.position, new_chapters.id, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
FROM new_chapters JOIN (VALUES
    (1, 1, 'Conditional Statements', 'Choose a branch with if, else if, and else.',
     '<h2>Making decisions in Java</h2><p>An if statement executes its body when a boolean expression is true. An else branch handles the remaining case. Use braces around each body to make the scope clear.</p><pre>int score = 82;
if (score &gt;= 90) {
    System.out.println("Excellent");
} else if (score &gt;= 60) {
    System.out.println("Passed");
} else {
    System.out.println("Try again");
}</pre><p>Branches are checked from top to bottom. Once a condition is true, later branches are skipped. Put more specific conditions before broader ones. Combine conditions with &amp;&amp; for AND and || for OR. These operators short-circuit: the right operand is evaluated only when needed.</p><h3>Practice</h3><p>Write a method that accepts a score from 0 to 100 and returns a grade. Reject values outside the range. Check boundary values such as 0, 59, 60, 89, 90, and 100.</p>'),
    (1, 2, 'Loops and Iteration', 'Repeat work using for and while loops.',
     '<h2>Repeating operations</h2><p>A for loop is useful when the number of iterations is known. Its header contains initialization, a condition checked before each iteration, and an update executed after the body.</p><pre>int sum = 0;
for (int number = 1; number &lt;= 5; number++) {
    sum += number;
}
System.out.println(sum); // 15</pre><p>A while loop checks its condition before the body and may execute zero times. A do-while loop checks after the body and executes at least once. Ensure the loop changes the state used in its condition so that it terminates. Use break to exit a loop and continue to skip the rest of the current iteration.</p><h3>Practice</h3><p>Compute the sum of even numbers from 1 to a supplied positive limit. Verify limits 1, 2, and 10; the expected sums are 0, 2, and 30.</p>'),
    (2, 1, 'Classes and Objects', 'Group state and behavior in a class.',
     '<h2>Defining a class</h2><p>A class describes the state and behavior of objects. Each object has its own instance fields. A constructor initializes a new object and has the same name as the class, with no return type.</p><pre>public class Course {
    private final String name;

    public Course(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }
}</pre><p>Create an instance with new Course("Java Developer"). The this keyword refers to the current object and distinguishes a field from a parameter with the same name. A final field can be assigned only once. Reference variables can refer to the same object, so changes to a mutable object may be visible through multiple references.</p><h3>Practice</h3><p>Create two Course instances with different names and print their names. Explain why each instance keeps its own field value.</p>'),
    (2, 2, 'Encapsulation and Validation', 'Protect object invariants through a small public interface.',
     '<h2>Keeping objects valid</h2><p>Encapsulation places implementation details behind an interface. Private fields prevent callers from changing state directly. Methods should express supported operations and reject values that would leave an object invalid.</p><pre>public void rename(String name) {
    if (name == null || name.isBlank()) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Name must not be blank");
    }
    this.name = name.trim();
}</pre><p>The null check comes first because || skips the right side when the left side is true. Apply the same validation during construction so that every instance starts valid. A getter does not always mean data is protected: returning a mutable collection can let callers change internal state. Prefer a copy or an unmodifiable view when appropriate.</p><h3>Practice</h3><p>Add rename to a course class. Check that a valid name is trimmed and that null, empty, and whitespace-only values are rejected without changing the previous name.</p>')
) AS data(chapter_position, position, name, description, content)
ON new_chapters.sort_order = data.chapter_position;
